---

<Article
  title="Reading the label on an alternator | Parts Relay"
  description="Identify the alternator markings a supplier needs when a label is worn or only part of the reference is legible."
  heading="Reading the label on an alternator"
  topic="Distributor counter"
  topicHref="/journal/"
  readTime="3 min read"
  relatedHeading="Other identification problems"
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    Two alternators can have a similar housing and pulley. If both appear against the same vehicle,
    a legible marking on the old unit can narrow the search. First ask the counter which marking it
    needs.
  </Fragment>
  <div
    slot="cover"
    class="lib-art lib-art-alt"
    aria-label="Technical illustration of an alternator"
    role="img"
  >
    <span>Electrical</span>
    <img src="/assets/alternator.svg" alt="" />
    <b>ALTERNATOR</b>
  </div>
  <section class="lib-case">
    <h2>What to record from a worn label</h2>
    <p>
      Write the rating and the part number on separate lines. A partial number is still useful if
      the counter can tell where it stops.
    </p>
    <dl class="lib-evidence">
      <div>
        <dt>Output rating</dt>
        <dd>
          Copy the amperage as printed, including the unit. The rating belongs in its own field,
          even when the manufacturer’s number is illegible.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Part number</dt>
        <dd>
          Read the characters in order and mark anything you couldn’t make out. A cropped photo can
          help the counter see whether a character is worn away or hidden at the edge.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Outstanding check</dt>
        <dd>
          Ask the supplier which remaining detail would settle the match. Write down the question in
          their words so the technician can check it in one visit.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Which number do you need?</h2>
    <p>
      The output rating and the manufacturer’s reference answer different questions. If the counter
      asks for the reference, copy the whole string, including letters, leading zeroes and the
      suffix. If one character is unclear, mark its position and leave it open.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>The plug can help</h2>
    <p>
      When the label is damaged, ask whether the connector shape distinguishes the remaining units.
      The counter person can tell the technician what to look for before anyone goes back to the
      vehicle.
    </p>
  </section>
  <p>
    <a class="lib-inline-link" href="/distributors/">
      How a distributor handles an open enquiry
      <svg
        aria-hidden="true"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.55"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      >
        <path d="M4 12h15m-6-6 6 6-6 6" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </p>
  <p class="lib-product-note">
    Parts Relay finds the parts from a plate or VIN and keeps each search in one queue the whole
    counter can see.
    <a class="lib-inline-link" href="/about/">
      How Parts Relay works
    </a>
  </p>
</Article>
